---
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro'
import { resolvePrivacyPublicationDate } from '../privacy-publication'
import { company, contact, googlePolicies, site } from '../site'

const sections = [
  { id: 'scope', title: 'Who we are and what this policy covers' },
  { id: 'information-we-collect', title: 'Information we collect' },
  { id: 'how-we-use-information', title: 'How we use information' },
  { id: 'google-user-data', title: 'Google user data' },
  { id: 'microsoft-calendar-data', title: 'Microsoft sign-in and calendar data' },
  { id: 'ai-assistants', title: 'AI assistants and API access' },
  { id: 'sharing', title: 'How we share information' },
  { id: 'invitees', title: 'If you book a meeting through Hourmere' },
  { id: 'self-hosted', title: 'Self-hosted Hourmere' },
  { id: 'retention', title: 'Retention and deletion' },
  { id: 'security', title: 'Security' },
  { id: 'international-transfers', title: 'Where data is processed' },
  { id: 'your-rights', title: 'Your rights and choices' },
  { id: 'this-website', title: 'This website, cookies and local storage' },
  { id: 'children', title: 'Children' },
  { id: 'changes', title: 'Changes to this policy' },
  { id: 'contact', title: 'Contact' },
]

const privacyMail = `mailto:${contact.privacy}`
const publicationDate = resolvePrivacyPublicationDate(import.meta.env.PRIVACY_PUBLICATION_DATE)
---

<Legal
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="How Hourmere, operated by Cobaltmere, LLC, collects, uses, stores, shares and deletes personal data, including data from Google and Microsoft."
  path="/privacy"
  effective={publicationDate}
  lastUpdated
  sections={sections}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      This Privacy Policy explains how {company.name} ("{company.shortName}", "we", "us") collects,
      uses, stores, shares and deletes personal data in connection with Hourmere: the website at
      hourmere.com, the Hourmere cloud service that we host at app.hourmere.com ("Hourmere Cloud"), and its API and
      remote MCP server (together, the "Service").
    </p>
    <p class="callout">
      Hourmere is in development. Hourmere Cloud, the hosted service we operate at
      <a href="https://app.hourmere.com">app.hourmere.com</a>, is currently an invitation-only preview
      while we prepare its public launch. This policy applies to that preview, including any
      Google user data it receives, and to this website.
    </p>
    <section class="summary" aria-labelledby="summary-title">
      <h2 id="summary-title">The short version</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We use your data to run Hourmere and to provide the scheduling features you use.</li>
        <li>
          Calendar data is used only for the connection and scheduling features described below.
          We never sell it, never use it for advertising and never use it to train
          generalized AI or machine learning models.
        </li>
        <li>
          AI assistants can access Hourmere through an API key you create or an MCP OAuth connection
          you approve, within your permissions and the selected organization.
        </li>
        <li>
          If you self-host Hourmere, its operator handles your data and any connected providers;
          Cobaltmere does not receive instance data automatically.
        </li>
        <li>
          You can disconnect providers and revoke agent access. You can request a copy or deletion
          of your data by email.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="scope">1. Who we are and what this policy covers</h2>
  <p>
    Hourmere is open-source scheduling software developed by {company.name}, a Delaware limited
    liability company. {company.name} operates Hourmere Cloud; self-hosted instances are operated by
    whoever runs them. For Hourmere Cloud, the instance operator is Cobaltmere.
    For Hourmere Cloud and this website, this policy
    replaces the general privacy policy at <a href={company.privacyPolicy}>cobaltmere.com/privacy</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    For Hourmere Cloud, {company.shortName} is responsible for the personal data described in this
    policy, except where we process data on behalf of our customers as described in
    <a href="#invitees">section 8</a>. Customers who need a data processing agreement, for example
    under the GDPR, can request one at <a href={privacyMail}>{contact.privacy}</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    This policy does not cover Hourmere installations that other people or organizations run on
    their own servers (see <a href="#self-hosted">section 9</a>), or services from third parties
    that you connect to Hourmere, such as your calendar provider or your AI assistant.
  </p>

  <h2 id="information-we-collect">2. Information we collect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Account information:</strong> your name, email address, time zone and settings.
      Hourmere accounts have no passwords. You sign in with a link sent to your email address, with
      Google or with Microsoft.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Email sign-in:</strong> when you sign in with an email link, we process your email
      address and a one-time sign-in token that expires after 5 minutes. Queued sign-in emails
      store the recipient and link encrypted. The queued copy is deleted after delivery, or when
      expiry is detected by the delivery worker or periodic cleanup.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sign-in with Google or Microsoft:</strong> we receive your name, email address and
      your Google account ID or Microsoft account identifier; Google may also provide a profile
      picture. We use them to create your account and sign you in, and as your account profile:
      your name (and picture, if shown) appears in Hourmere, on your booking pages and in booking
      emails, and we send account and booking emails to your email address. Google and Microsoft
      sign-in access and refresh tokens are stored encrypted; the ID token returned at sign-in is
      not stored.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Calendar connection information:</strong> when you connect a Google or Microsoft
      calendar, the access and refresh tokens the provider issues to Hourmere, and the email address
      of the connected account.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Calendar data:</strong> Google calendar metadata, event identifiers, times and status
      used to calculate busy intervals, sync metadata, and the events Hourmere writes for bookings.
      Microsoft connections currently retrieve account and calendar-list metadata only. See
      <a href="#google-user-data">section 4</a> and
      <a href="#microsoft-calendar-data">section 5</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Scheduling information:</strong> the event types, availability settings and booking pages
      you set up.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Booking information:</strong> details that invitees enter when they book with you,
      such as their name, email address and notes, and the time, time zone and status of each
      booking.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>API and agent access:</strong> hashed API keys, MCP OAuth client and consent records,
      the organization you authorize, tokens needed for OAuth connections, and booking records
      identifying the credential used to create, reschedule or cancel a booking. MCP OAuth access
      tokens expire after 5 minutes; refresh tokens expire after 30 days and rotate when used.
      When MCP OAuth is enabled, a background cleanup runs every 15 minutes and deletes OAuth
      access and refresh tokens one day after they expire, expired authorization attempts and
      client assertions, authorization grants left unfinished for more than one day, and
      dynamically registered clients that did not complete consent within 24 hours or have been
      unused for more than 90 days.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Conference information:</strong> for automatic Google Meet or Zoom, the provider and
      organizer identifiers, the organizer name and email address used for invitations, meeting or
      event identifiers, provisioning status and participant join link. Zoom connections also store
      account display information and encrypted access and refresh tokens. See
      <a href="#automatic-conferences">section 7</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Webhook information:</strong> organization-configured destination URLs, encrypted
      signing secrets, minimal booking-event snapshots and delivery status. See
      <a href="#booking-webhooks">section 7</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Background jobs:</strong> job payloads contain record IDs and job-control fields,
      rather than message contents, booking answers, management links or credentials. Completed
      job records are deleted after 7 days by default; sign-in-email job records are deleted sooner.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Billing information</strong> (paid plans only): your plan, number of seats, billing
      country and transaction records. When you buy a paid plan, payments are processed by Stripe;
      for purchases made through Stripe's merchant-of-record service, your order is sold through
      Link, LLC, an affiliate of Stripe, which handles payment processing and applicable sales tax,
      VAT or GST. Card details are collected and processed by Stripe or Link, LLC, and we do not
      store full card numbers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Usage and diagnostic information:</strong> server and API log data such as IP
      address, browser and device type, the pages and API endpoints requested, timestamps and error
      reports.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Communications:</strong> messages you send to us, for example to our support address
      or to ask for launch updates.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="callout">
    Hourmere Cloud is in an invitation-only preview and takes no payments yet; the billing description above
    applies once paid plans are available.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-we-use-information">3. How we use information</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      To provide the Service: signing you in, computing your availability, showing your booking
      pages, creating, updating and cancelling calendar events for bookings, sending email
      confirmations and reminders, and carrying out requests from applications you have authorized.
    </li>
    <li>To keep the Service secure, prevent fraud and abuse, and find and fix problems.</li>
    <li>To process payments for paid plans.</li>
    <li>
      To answer your questions, send you service-related notices, and send the launch announcement
      if you asked for launch updates.
    </li>
    <li>
      To understand how the Service is used and improve it, using usage and diagnostic information.
    </li>
    <li>To comply with legal obligations and enforce our terms.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We do not sell personal data. We do not use personal data for advertising, and we do not use
    calendar data or booking information to train generalized AI or machine learning models. We send
    marketing emails only if you ask to receive them.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are in the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom or Switzerland, our legal bases
    for processing are: performance of our contract with you, which covers providing the Service,
    including billing for paid plans;
    our legitimate interests in operating a secure and reliable service, which cover security,
    fraud and abuse prevention, and service improvement; compliance with legal obligations, which
    covers tax and accounting records; and your consent, which covers marketing emails and any
    other processing for which we ask your permission. You can withdraw consent at any time; see
    <a href="#your-rights">section 13</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="google-user-data">4. Google user data</h2>
  <p>
    This section describes how Hourmere accesses, uses, stores, shares and deletes data it receives
    from Google when you sign in with Google or connect Google Calendar ("Google user data").
    Signing in with Google and connecting Google Calendar are separate, optional steps. Signing in
    does not give Hourmere access to your calendar; Hourmere asks for calendar access only when you
    choose to connect Google Calendar.
  </p>

  <h3>Signing in with Google</h3>
  <p>
    If you sign in with Google, Hourmere receives your name, email address, profile picture (if
    available) and Google account ID. We use them to create your account and sign you in, and as
    your account profile: your name (and picture, if shown) appears in Hourmere, on your booking
    pages and in booking emails, and we send account and booking emails to your email address.
  </p>

  <h3>Google Calendar: what we access</h3>
  <p>
    Depending on the permissions you grant on Google's consent screen, Hourmere accesses:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the email address of your Google account, to show you which account is connected;</li>
    <li>
      the list of calendars in your account (such as their names, identifiers and time zones), so
      you can choose which calendars to check for conflicts and which calendar receives new
      bookings;
    </li>
    <li>
      event identifiers, start and end times, cancellation and busy/free status, and your own
      attendance status on the calendars you select for conflict checks. Hourmere uses these to
      calculate and store busy intervals, with event identifiers and sync metadata needed to keep
      them up to date. Busy-time synchronization does not request or store event titles,
      descriptions, locations or attendee names or email addresses; and
    </li>
    <li>
      identifiers and status of the booking events Hourmere writes to your selected destination
      calendar, so it can reconcile updates and cancellations. The ordinary booking copy contains
      the booking title, which includes the invitee's name, start and end times, calendar UID,
      revision and status. It does not send email addresses, guest names, attendee lists, booking
      answers or private management links to Google. Automatic Google Meet and optional calendar
      copies with join links are described in
      <a href="#automatic-conferences">section 7</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We request only the permissions these features need. Google's consent screen shows the exact
    permissions before you agree.
  </p>
  <h3>Google permissions Hourmere requests</h3>
  <p>
    Sign in with Google requests <code>openid</code>, <code>email</code> and <code>profile</code>
    (your Google account ID, email address, name and profile picture) to create your account and
    sign you in; it does not give Hourmere calendar access. Connecting Google Calendar is a
    separate step that requests:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>openid</code> and <code>email</code>, to identify the connected Google account;
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.calendarlist.readonly</code>, to list your
      calendars with their names, identifiers, primary-calendar status, time zones, your access
      level and whether Google Meet is available, so you can choose calendars;
    </li>
    <li>
      when you enable synchronization,
      <code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.events.readonly</code>, used only to read event
      identifiers, start and end times, status, busy/free setting and your own response for events
      on the calendars you select for conflict checks; and
    </li>
    <li>
      when you enable synchronization,
      <code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.events.owned</code>, used only to create,
      update and cancel the booking events Hourmere adds to the calendar you own and select for
      new bookings, to add a Google Meet link to those events when you turn on automatic Google
      Meet, and to read those booking events back to keep them in sync.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How we use it</h3>
  <p>
    We use Google user data only to provide Hourmere's user-facing features. We use sign-in data to
    create your account, sign you in and show your account profile, as described above. We use
    calendar data only for scheduling: showing your available times on your booking pages and to
    applications you authorize, preventing double-booking, adding booked meetings to your calendar,
    updating or removing those events when bookings change, and showing you, and applications you
    authorize, the status of your calendar connection.
  </p>
  <p>We do not:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>sell Google user data;</li>
    <li>
      use or transfer it for advertising, including retargeting and personalized or interest-based
      advertising;
    </li>
    <li>transfer it to advertising platforms, data brokers or information resellers;</li>
    <li>use it to determine credit-worthiness or for lending purposes; or</li>
    <li>
      use or transfer it to develop, improve or train generalized or non-personalized AI or machine
      learning models.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Hourmere does not use data obtained through Google Workspace APIs to develop, improve, or
    train generalized or non-personalized AI and/or ML models.
  </p>

  <h3>How we share it</h3>
  <p>We do not share or transfer Google user data, except:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      as needed to provide the features you use. For example, people who visit your booking page
      see the times you are available, but not the details of your calendar events, and an AI
      assistant or other application you authorize receives, at your direction, the results of the
      requests it makes for you, such as your availability or your connected Google account's email
      address and calendar names (see <a href="#ai-assistants">section 6</a>);
    </li>
    <li>
      with service providers that host and run the Service for us, such as our cloud hosting and
      database providers, who may process it only on our instructions and only to provide the
      Service;
    </li>
    <li>when necessary for security purposes, such as investigating abuse;</li>
    <li>to comply with applicable laws; or</li>
    <li>
      as part of a merger, acquisition or sale of assets, only after obtaining your explicit prior
      consent.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Who can read it</h3>
  <p>
    Our staff do not read your Google user data unless you give us explicit permission for specific
    data (for example, to help with a support request), it is necessary for security purposes (such
    as investigating a bug or abuse), it is necessary to comply with applicable law, or the data
    has been aggregated and anonymized and is used for internal operations in line with applicable
    law.
  </p>

  <h3>How we store and protect it</h3>
  <p>
    Hourmere Cloud, including the preview at app.hourmere.com, runs on a VPS in Los Angeles,
    United States, operated by BandwagonHost. Google user data is stored in Hourmere's database
    on that server and in its on-host backups (see <a href="#retention">section 10</a>).
    Google user data is encrypted in transit. Google Calendar connection access and refresh
    tokens are stored encrypted with AES-256-GCM; Google sign-in access and refresh tokens are
    also stored encrypted. Access is limited to the systems and
    personnel that need it to run the Service. See <a href="#security">section 11</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Retention and deletion</h3>
  <p>
    We keep Google user data only as long as we need it to provide the features described above.
    While synchronization is active, Hourmere reads busy intervals for a rolling window from
    three days before the current UTC day to 403 days (about 13 months) ahead. It normally checks
    selected calendars about every minute using incremental updates and rebuilds the window
    daily. Events overlapping the window can start before it or end after it. If synchronization
    stops, including when you turn off conflict checks, stored busy intervals and sync data remain
    until the calendar is synchronized again, the connection is disconnected in Hourmere, or you
    ask us to delete them.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>When you disconnect Google Calendar,</strong> Hourmere deletes the local connection,
      encrypted tokens, calendar list, busy intervals and dependent sync and event mappings from
      its active database, and stops using that connection. This does not automatically revoke
      Google's account-wide grant; remove Hourmere separately in your Google Account's third-party
      connections if you want to revoke that grant.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your Hourmere booking records and automatic conference records</strong> are separate
      from the connection. Disconnecting does not delete them or an existing join link. Retention
      and deletion requests are described in <a href="#retention">section 10</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Events that Hourmere created in your Google Calendar</strong> remain at Google until
      you delete them there.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Deletion requests:</strong> you can ask about or request deletion of the Google
      sign-in data and other account data we hold by emailing
      <a href={privacyMail}>{contact.privacy}</a>; we respond within one month. See
      <a href="#retention">section 10</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You can also remove Hourmere's access at any time from your Google Account's <a
      href={googlePolicies.connections}>third-party connections page</a
    >, and you can ask us to delete your data by emailing <a href={privacyMail}>{contact.privacy}</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you remove Hourmere's access in your Google Account, Hourmere can no longer read or update
    your calendar. When Hourmere detects the authorization failure, it marks the connection as
    needing reconnection; its stored connection data remains until you disconnect it in Hourmere
    or ask us to delete it.
  </p>

  <h3>Limited Use</h3>
  <p class="callout">
    Hourmere's use and transfer to any other app of information received from Google APIs will
    adhere to the <a href={googlePolicies.userData}>Google API Services User Data Policy</a>,
    including the Limited Use requirements. Hourmere's use of information received from Google
    Workspace APIs, including the Google Calendar API, also adheres to the <a
      href={googlePolicies.workspace}>Google Workspace API User Data and Developer Policy</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p>
    If we change how Hourmere uses Google user data, we will tell you and ask for your consent
    before using it in a new way or for a different purpose.
  </p>

  <h2 id="microsoft-calendar-data">5. Microsoft sign-in and calendar data</h2>
  <p>
    If you sign in with Microsoft, Hourmere receives your name, email address and Microsoft account
    identifier. The current Microsoft sign-in configuration does not request a profile photo. We
    use this information to create your account, sign you in and show your account profile; your
    name appears on booking pages and in booking emails, and we send account and booking emails to
    your email address. Signing in does not give Hourmere access to your calendar.
  </p>
  <p>
    Connecting Microsoft Calendar is a separate, optional authorization. Hourmere currently reads
    the connected account identifier and email address, and a calendar list containing identifiers,
    names, default-calendar status and access permissions. It requests basic calendar and offline
    access to list calendars and refresh credentials. Although that provider permission can allow
    access to basic event metadata, the current integration does not read event times or busy
    intervals, synchronize events, or write booking events to Microsoft.
  </p>
  <p>
    Microsoft connection access and refresh tokens are stored encrypted on the server. We use
    Microsoft data only for the features described here, never sell it, never use it for advertising
    and never use it to train generalized AI or machine learning models. Disconnecting deletes the
    local connection, encrypted credentials, calendar metadata and outstanding connection attempts
    from the active database. It does not automatically revoke the provider-side grant. Remove
    that grant separately in your Microsoft account's app permissions, or ask your organization's
    administrator. Retention and deletion requests are described in
    <a href="#retention">section 10</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-assistants">6. AI assistants and API access</h2>
  <p>
    Hourmere has an API and a remote MCP server that AI assistants and other applications can use.
    You can authorize them with an API key you create or, when enabled by the instance operator,
    an MCP OAuth connection you approve while signed in. Both act as you in the selected
    organization, subject to your current permissions. API keys can use permitted REST and MCP
    operations, including changing your calendar conflict and destination settings and refreshing
    or syncing your calendars. MCP OAuth permission covers the available MCP tools, including
    reading your profile, event-type configurations, working-hours schedules, hosted bookings with
    attendee details and your calendar and Zoom connection details (connected account email
    addresses, calendar names and settings, Zoom display name and connection status, without
    credentials), checking availability, and creating, rescheduling or cancelling bookings. MCP
    OAuth does not grant REST API access or account or API-key administration. If you approve offline
    access, the connector can refresh its access without asking you to sign in for each request.
  </p>
  <p>
    Availability, booking and connection data that Hourmere returns to an AI agent or application
    you authorized is sent at your direction, to provide the feature you requested. The availability is calculated
    from your connected calendars. Our commitments about Google user data in
    <a href="#google-user-data">section 4</a> (no advertising, no sale, and no use by us to train AI
    or machine learning models) apply to Hourmere. Once the data is delivered to the application,
    its handling is governed by the terms and privacy policy of the application's provider, and of
    the AI model behind it, not by this policy. Only authorize agents and applications you trust.
    You can revoke an API key on the API keys page or an MCP OAuth grant in Connected apps.
    Revocation stops future requests using that credential; it does not retrieve information the
    application has already received.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sharing">7. How we share information</h2>
  <p>
    We do not sell personal data. We share it only as described in this policy, including with:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Service providers</strong> that act only on our instructions, under contracts that
      require them to protect the data: cloud hosting and database providers, and an email delivery
      provider for sign-in links, booking confirmations and reminders.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Payment providers:</strong> when you buy a paid plan, payments are processed by
      Stripe; for purchases made through Stripe's merchant-of-record service, your order is sold
      through Link, LLC, an affiliate of Stripe, which handles payment processing and applicable
      sales tax, VAT or GST. Stripe and Link, LLC also act as independent controllers for their own
      purposes, such as fraud prevention and meeting their legal and regulatory obligations, under
      their own privacy policies, including
      <a href="https://stripe.com/privacy">Stripe's privacy policy</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Calendar and conference providers you connect:</strong> Google receives the calendar
      and automatic Meet requests described in sections 4 and 7; Microsoft currently receives
      account and calendar-list requests as described in section 5; Zoom receives the automatic
      meeting requests described below.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Applications you authorize</strong>, such as AI assistants, as described in
      <a href="#ai-assistants">section 6</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The other party to a booking.</strong> When someone books with you, the details they
      enter are shared with you, and your name and the booking details are shared with them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Legal and safety reasons:</strong> when required by law, or to protect the rights,
      property or safety of our users, the public or {company.shortName}.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Business transfers:</strong> if {company.shortName} is involved in a merger,
      acquisition or sale of assets, subject to this policy. For Google user data, we will first
      obtain your explicit consent.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="callout">
    Hourmere Cloud is in an invitation-only preview and takes no payments yet; the payment-provider description
    above applies once paid plans are available.
  </p>

  <h3 id="automatic-conferences">Automatic Google Meet and Zoom</h3>
  <p>
    Automatic meeting creation requires an explicit event-type opt-in and the organizer's
    authorized provider connection. For Google Meet, Hourmere sends Google the booking title,
    which includes the invitee's name, start and end times, calendar UID, revision and status, and
    a conference-creation request identifier on the selected organizer's calendar. It does not send
    email addresses, guest names, attendee lists, booking answers or management links in these
    requests. Rescheduling updates the pinned event; cancellation asks Google to cancel that event.
    Cancelling an event does not guarantee that access to an existing Meet link is revoked.
  </p>
  <p>
    Zoom authorization is separate from sign-in and calendar connections. Connecting an identity
    reads the Zoom account identifier and, when available, email and display name. Meeting creation
    requires a separate authorization to create, read, update and delete meetings. Hourmere sends
    Zoom the booking title, which includes the invitee's name, as the meeting topic, start time,
    duration and the UTC time zone, with settings to use a scheduled meeting rather than a personal
    meeting ID and request a generated passcode. These requests do not send email addresses, guest
    names, attendee lists, booking answers or management links. Hourmere stores Zoom connection
    tokens encrypted and retains the provider identifiers needed to update the same meeting on
    reschedule or request deletion on cancellation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hourmere stores the validated participant join link with the protected booking when the meeting
    is ready. A Zoom participant link may include a passcode. Authorized booking views, invitee
    management links and participant emails and ICS attachments can reveal the join link; an
    explicitly configured ordinary Google calendar copy can also contain it. Public event-type
    catalogs and webhook payloads do not include it, and Hourmere does not place it in logs, job
    payloads or browser storage. Hourmere does not retain Zoom host start links or standalone
    provider passcodes or PINs. Google and Zoom handle meetings and any information you give them under their own terms
    and privacy policies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Disconnecting Zoom clears local credentials and pending authorization attempts, while keeping
    connection identity metadata and existing booking and conference records. It does not
    automatically revoke Zoom's provider-side grant or delete existing provider meetings. A
    disconnected provider can prevent later updates or remote cancellation; manage the grant and
    any remaining meetings with the provider. Retention of remaining Hourmere records is
    described in <a href="#retention">section 10</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    When the instance operator has configured the signed Zoom event endpoint and Zoom delivers
    an app deauthorization notification, removing the app in Zoom also deletes the matching
    local Zoom connection credentials and identity metadata and clears pending authorization
    attempts. Existing booking and conference records remain subject to the retention described
    in <a href="#retention">section 10</a>; the notification does not delete meetings held by Zoom.
  </p>

  <h3 id="booking-webhooks">Organization booking webhooks</h3>
  <p>
    If the instance operator enables webhooks, organization owners and administrators can configure
    recipients for signed HTTPS notifications of booking creation, rescheduling and cancellation
    across their organization. The payload contains event, booking, organization and event-type
    identifiers, timestamps, booking status and source, and a predecessor booking identifier for
    rescheduling. It does not include names, email addresses, attendees, booking answers, meeting
    locations or join links, private management links, API keys or OAuth credentials. The
    organization chooses the receiving services and is responsible for how they use those
    notifications. Hourmere stores the minimal payload and delivery status to deliver and retry it.
    A background cleanup deletes webhook delivery records, including their payloads, after 30 days
    from creation. Deleting the endpoint deletes its delivery records sooner.
  </p>

  <h3>Email notifications and calendar attachments</h3>
  <p>
    Hourmere sends sign-in emails and booking confirmations, changes, cancellations and reminders
    through the instance operator's configured email service. Ordinary booking messages go to current
    eligible hosts, the invitee and any guests added when the host permits them. Host emails
    include the invitee's contact details, guests and answers; private management links go only to
    the invitee. Confirmations, reschedules and cancellations can carry ICS calendar attachments
    containing the meeting title, times, location or ready join link, and organizer and attendee
    names and email addresses. Those attachments do not contain private management links or booking
    answers. Automatic meeting invitations wait for a ready join link; a provisioning-failure
    notice has no invitation attachment. Email services and recipients' mail and calendar systems
    process these messages and attachments.
  </p>

  <h2 id="invitees">8. If you book a meeting through Hourmere</h2>
  <p>
    If you book a meeting on someone's Hourmere booking page, the person or organization you are
    booking with (the "host") decides what information to ask for and how to use it. We process
    your booking information on the host's behalf to provide the Service, for example to add the
    meeting to the host's calendar and send you confirmations and reminders. For questions about
    how a host uses your information, contact the host. If you contact us, we will pass your
    request on or help where we can.
  </p>

  <h2 id="self-hosted">9. Self-hosted Hourmere</h2>
  <p>
    Hourmere is designed so that anyone can run it on their own servers. A self-hosted instance is
    operated by whoever runs it, and that operator is responsible for the data it holds and for its
    connections to calendar, conference and email providers, webhook recipients and authorized
    agents. {company.name} does not automatically receive data from self-hosted instances. The
    flows described here explain software features; a self-hosted operator chooses which to
    enable and supplies its own privacy information, provider configuration, retention and
    handling of data requests. Contact that operator about data held by its instance. If we ever
    add telemetry to the software, it will be optional, opt-in and anonymous, and we will document
    what it sends.
  </p>

  <h2 id="retention">10. Retention and deletion</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Account, scheduling and booking information</strong> is kept while your account
      exists. Disconnecting a calendar removes its local connection data; cancelling a booking
      keeps its booking record and is not a deletion request.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Calendar data</strong> is retained and deleted as described in section 4 (Google) and
      section 5 (Microsoft).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Requests for launch updates</strong> are kept until you ask us to stop or we have sent
      the launch announcement.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Beyond the cleanup described in sections 2, 4 and 7 and the backup rotation below, Hourmere does not currently run other
    automatic deletion schedules for account data, conference identity and meeting records,
    remaining OAuth and notification metadata, server and API logs or support correspondence.
    You can ask about or request deletion of the data {company.shortName} holds
    about you by emailing <a href={privacyMail}>{contact.privacy}</a>; we respond within one
    month. Data already sent to calendar, conference and email providers, webhook recipients or
    applications you authorized is handled under their own policies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hourmere Cloud makes daily on-host backups, kept for 7 days, and keeps 4 weekly copies.
    These backups rotate automatically; deleted data may remain in a retained backup until that
    copy is rotated out. Off-site backups are not yet in place.
  </p>

  <h2 id="security">11. Security</h2>
  <p>
    We protect personal data with measures appropriate to the risk, including encryption in transit,
    encrypted storage of Google and Microsoft sign-in access and refresh tokens, calendar and
    Zoom connection tokens, queued sign-in emails and webhook signing secrets, hashed API keys,
    access controls and the principle of least
    privilege. No system is completely secure. If a data breach affects your personal data, we will
    notify you as required by law. To report a security issue, email <a
      href={`mailto:${contact.security}`}>{contact.security}</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2 id="international-transfers">12. Where data is processed</h2>
  <p>
    We are based in the United States. Hourmere Cloud, including the invitation-only preview at
    app.hourmere.com, runs on a VPS in Los Angeles, United States, operated by BandwagonHost.
    Hourmere Cloud data and its on-host backups are stored there. The hourmere.com website is published as static
    files on Cloudflare Workers; the request data its hosting processes is described in
    <a href="#this-website">section 14</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="your-rights">13. Your rights and choices</h2>
  <p>
    Depending on where you live, you may have the right to access, correct, delete or export your
    personal data, to restrict or object to its processing, and to withdraw consent at any time. You
    can disconnect calendar and Zoom connections, revoke API keys and revoke MCP OAuth grants in
    the Service. To request deletion, a copy or export of your data, or to exercise any of these
    rights, email
    <a href={privacyMail}>{contact.privacy}</a>. We will not discriminate against you for exercising
    them. You may also lodge a complaint with your local data protection authority.
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not sell personal information or share it for cross-context behavioral advertising, as
    those terms are defined under California law.
  </p>

  <h2 id="this-website">14. This website, cookies and local storage</h2>
  <p>
    hourmere.com is a static website. It does not set cookies itself; our hosting provider may set
    strictly necessary security cookies. The website does not use analytics or third-party trackers,
    and its fonts are served from hourmere.com. Our website hosting provider processes standard
    request data, such as IP address, browser type and the page requested, to deliver the site and
    protect it from abuse. The documentation pages save small preferences, such as your light or
    dark theme choice, in your browser's local storage; this stays on your device.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hourmere Cloud, including the preview, uses only cookies that are strictly necessary for it to
    work, such as keeping you signed in. We do not use advertising cookies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Booking widgets display an Hourmere booking page in an isolated frame on a website allowed by
    the instance operator, inline or in a popup. Booking details you enter are sent directly to
    that Hourmere instance through its public booking API; the widget does not require an account
    or third-party cookies. Messages from the frame to the surrounding website contain only a
    correlation identifier, height or completion/close signal, not booking identifiers, names,
    email addresses, answers, management tokens or join links. The surrounding website has its own
    privacy practices, so use a host website you trust. Hourmere may record the booking source as
    an embed; this is not visitor tracking or proof of the embedding website's identity.
  </p>

  <h2 id="children">15. Children</h2>
  <p>
    Hourmere is not directed to children. You must be at least 18 years old, or the age of majority
    where you live if that is higher, to create a Hourmere account. We do not knowingly collect
    personal data from children. If you believe a child has given us personal data, contact us and
    we will delete it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">16. Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    We may update this policy. We will post the new version on this page with a new effective date
    and, if the changes are material, notify you by email or in the Service before they take effect.
    Changes to how we use Google user data are also subject to section 4.
  </p>

